# Core invariants (joinUp)

These do not map cleanly to a single file glob, which is exactly why they are easy to miss if rules
are only loaded by glob match. Treat this file as always in scope.

## Auth & security
- Chat write paths must use `authenticateToken` / `socket.userId` + `checkChatPermission`.
- Never trust a client-supplied `userId`. Never fail-open a socket JWT check. Never leave
  `POST /api/messages` public.
- `req.user.isAdmin` comes from Clerk metadata (`isAdmin` / `role=admin`) or `ADMIN_USER_IDS`. Do not
  hardcode `false` (or `true`).

## Transactions
- Roster joins, waitlist promotion, and game creation must be atomic.
- Game + group chat pairing must happen in the same interactive transaction, where the group chat
  `id` exactly equals the `game.id`.

## Prisma client lifecycle
- Import `prisma` from `server/lib/prisma.js`. Never instantiate `new PrismaClient()` inside an
  Express request handler, a loop, or a worker tick (one client per process).

## Background job idempotency
- Scheduler handlers must claim work with `updateMany` on sentinel fields (`lotteryExecutedAt`,
  `reminderSent`, `pickingOpenedAt`, `status`) so a second Render instance is a no-op.
- Set `RUN_BACKGROUND_JOBS=false` on extra API instances so they do not arm in-memory timers.

## Shared types
- Shared client/server contracts live in `shared/` (`@joinup/shared`). Do not copy timezone/sports/
  `Game` types between `next_app` and `mobile_app` — import the shared definition.

## Payments
- `Game.price` / `GameSeries.price` are display-only (pay at field). Do not add a payment processor
  without a product brief.

## Dev DB connection discipline (Neon autosuspend — blanket, applies to every agent/task)
The dev DB is Neon and autosuspends after 5 minutes with **zero** open connections. Every query —
even one from an idle-but-connected process — resets that timer, so the goal is not "spread work
out" (that repeatedly re-triggers the timer and never lets it fire) but **minimize total connected
time and cluster the connections you do need**:
- **Default the local Jest quality gate to the single new/changed test file**, not the full
  `server/tests/` suite (`npx jest <file> --runInBand`), per `L3_review.md`. The full suite (~20+
  files, 50-400+s depending on load) is for CI, which runs against its own disposable Postgres
  service (see `ci-cd.mdc`) — that path doesn't touch the dev DB's timer at all.
- **If you must run the full suite locally, don't deliberately delay or space it out** relative to
  other agents' runs — a gap that's shorter than 5 minutes just keeps the DB awake for longer with
  no benefit. If you know another agent is mid-run against the same dev DB, either wait for it to
  finish and run immediately after (back-to-back, not spaced), or skip the full local run and rely
  on CI's isolated Postgres instead.
- Don't hold a Prisma client / DB connection open across long-running non-DB work (long compiles,
  waiting on user input) — let it close so the timer can start.

## Isolation (blanket, applies to every agent/task)
**Never work directly in the shared main checkout.** Always create or reuse an isolated
`git worktree` (see `.claude/worktrees/agent-*` for the existing convention) before editing any
file, running installs, or running tests. Two agents editing/deleting files in the same physical
directory at the same time is not a hypothetical: it has already happened here — a concurrent
process working directly in the main checkout left the entire `shared/` package (used by both
`next_app` and `mobile_app` via `file:../shared`) deleted from disk mid-session, uncommitted,
with no replacement and no indication it was intentional. It was fully recoverable only because
git still had it tracked (`git checkout HEAD -- <path>`) and nothing had been committed over the
gap yet — do not rely on that luck happening again.
- If you find yourself about to run a destructive-adjacent command (deleting/moving a directory,
  a large rewrite, a dependency reinstall) and you are **not** in a dedicated worktree, stop and
  create one first.
- Commit early and often within your own worktree. The recovery above worked because the last
  known-good state was one git checkout away — an uncommitted change with no git history behind
  it is not recoverable the same way.
- Before committing, check `git status` for changes you did not make (especially large deletions)
  and leave them unstaged rather than folding them into your own commit — see the incident above.
